## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of reproducibility, determinism, and data lineage in a beginner-friendly setting. Emphasizes auditability and simple tests.

## Key Concepts
- Deterministic feature engineering
- Data lineage and reproducibility IDs
- Minimal, auditable API design
- Lightweight explainability vectors

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');
function computeReproScore(userId, region, modelVersion, seed) {
  const input = `${userId}|${region}|${modelVersion}|${seed}`;
  const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(input).digest('hex');
  const n = parseInt(hash.slice(0, 8), 16);
  return (n % 1000) / 10; // deterministic score 0-99.9
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift in reproducibility across model_version updates?
- How would you secure seeds and input digests to protect privacy while enabling audits?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design verifiable, privacy-preserving aggregation systems with end-to-end integrity guarantees using non-interactive zero-knowledge proofs. Candidates should outline the complete architecture, data flows, and anti-tampering mechanisms required for secure distributed computation.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-preserving aggregation with succinct non-interactive arguments of knowledge (SNARKs)
- Tamper-evident ledger chaining for auditability
- Replay protection through nonce management and commitment schemes
- Poisoning resistance in distributed share systems via consistency checks

## Code Example
```javascript
async function verifyProof(proof, publicInputs) {
  // SNARK verification stub
  // Validates that aggregate(sum of shares) == claimed_output
  // without revealing individual share contributions
  return snarklib.verify(verificationKey, publicInputs, proof);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
1. How would you handle device dropout during aggregation rounds?
2. What fallback mechanisms exist if SNARK verification fails?
3. How do you calibrate proof generation time against aggregation freshness requirements?

## Why This Is Asked
Gauges how well a candidate designs feature-level gating in a per-user scoring API, a practical knob for privacy, performance, and experimentation.

## Key Concepts
- Feature gating: filter features at the API boundary.
- Deterministic scoring: gating should not introduce nondeterminism.
- Validation & observability: log gating decisions for audits.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: apply feature gating before scoring
function computeScoreWithGating(allFeatures, allowedFeatures){
  const mask = new Set(allowedFeatures || allFeatures.map(f => f.name));
  const included = allFeatures.filter(f => mask.has(f.name));
  return computeScoreFromFeatures(included);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
